      "note": {
        "status": "reviewed",
        "audience": "generic-selfhosters",
        "exposure": "not-default",
        "assumptions": [
          "No row security policies (CREATE POLICY) are used to hide rows from database roles.",
          "Or every role with INSERT, UPDATE and SELECT on a table with such policies is trusted to read all of its rows."
        ],
        "actions": [
          {
            "kind": "backup"
          },
          {
            "kind": "upgrade",
            "text": "Move to 18.7, 17.12, 16.16, 15.20 or 14.25. On a floating tag such as postgres:17, pull the image again and recreate the container."
          },
          {
            "kind": "commands",
            "code": "docker compose pull\ndocker compose up -d\n"
          }
        ],
        "authorship": "human",
        "reviewed_by": "example-reviewer",
        "reviewed_at": "2026-11-13",
        "body": "This only matters if you rely on row security policies to stop a database role from reading some rows. A role that can INSERT, UPDATE and SELECT on such a table can read every row of it by repeating INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE.\n\nA typical self-hosted app database, with one application role and no row security policies, gains nothing from the fix, but the minor upgrade is routine: back up, pull the fixed image and recreate the container.\n"
      }
